data.dns-servers#DNS servers:
A server name (tag) must be unique: lowercase Latin letters, digits and _, starts with a letter, at most 24 characters.
Field Type Required Description tag string yes Server name (Name) type string no Server type (DNS type): static (Plaintext DNS, default) or keenetic (Keenetic DNS) address string for static IP address of the DNS server, with optional port, for example "10.8.0.1", "10.8.0.1:5353", "2001:4860:4860::8888", or "[2001:4860:4860::8888]:5353" detour string no Outbound the router itself uses to send queries to this server (Make requests via Outbound) detour is useful when a DNS server must be reached through a specific connection, usually the same VPN. It does not affect queries that devices in your network send directly to the same address: route them with a normal route rule.
config.json{ "dns": { "servers": [ { "tag": "vpn_dns", "type": "static", "address": "10.8.0.1:5353", "detour": "vpn" }, { "tag": "google_dns", "address": "8.8.8.8" }, { "tag": "google_dns_v6", "address": "[2001:4860:4860::8888]:53" }, { "tag": "keenetic_dns", "type": "keenetic" } ] } }

data.ignore#\u003ccode\u003eignore\u003c/code:
Use it for exceptions: traffic follows the router’s normal routing, as if keen-pbr did not touch it.
Rules are checked from top to bottom and the first match wins, so put the ignore rule above a broader one. For example, the whole streaming list goes through the VPN except addresses from the streaming_direct list (direct is the ignore outbound from the example below):
config.json"rules": [ { "list": ["streaming_direct"], "outbound": "direct" }, { "list": ["streaming"], "outbound": "vpn" } ] Field Type Required Description tag string yes Unique identifier type string yes "ignore" config.json{ "outbounds": [ { "type": "ignore", "tag": "direct" } ] }

data.table#\u003ccode\u003etable\u003c/code\u:
Use it when traffic must follow an existing routing table (a set of system routes): 254 is the main table, meaning the normal internet connection, or a table another service has created.
To see which tables exist and their names, run:
baship rule show cat /etc/iproute2/rt_tables Field Type Required Description tag string yes Unique identifier type string yes "table" table integer yes Existing routing table number. In the Web UI: Table ID config.json{ "outbounds": [ { "type": "table", "tag": "custom_table", "table": 200 } ] }

data.urltest#\u003ccode\u003eurltest\u003c/co:
Use it when you have several links (for example two VPNs and your normal internet) and want keen-pbr to pick a working one automatically. keen-pbr periodically opens url through each link and measures the response time. In the Web UI the type is called Auto-select (urltest).
If no link works, traffic for this outbound is blocked rather than leaking through your normal internet connection around the VPN.
Field Type Required Description tag string yes Unique identifier type string yes "urltest" url string yes URL used for availability and latency checks interval_ms integer no (default: 180000) Interval between checks in milliseconds probe_timeout_ms integer no (default: 5000) Timeout for one check in milliseconds tolerance_ms integer no (default: 100) Do not switch to another link if it is faster than the current one by less than this many milliseconds strategy string no (default: "priority") How to pick a link. In the Web UI: Selection strategy. "priority" (Fastest): all traffic goes through one link, the one with the lowest latency in the first working tier. "balance" (Multipath): new connections are spread across the working links by weight (not available on Keenetic) conntrack_on_switch string no (default: "preserve") What to do with open connections when switching to another link. In the Web UI: Existing connections on switch. "preserve" (Keep) keeps them on the old link, "delete" (Reset) deletes their conntrack entries so they reconnect. See Failover outbound_groups array yes Ordered list of link groups, called tiers in the Web UI (see below) retry object no Check retries (see below) circuit_breaker object no Temporary exclusion of an unstable link (see below) config.json{ "outbounds": [ { "type": "urltest", "tag": "auto_select", "url": "https://www.gstatic.com/generate_204", "interval_ms": 180000, "probe_timeout_ms": 5000, "tolerance_ms": 100, "outbound_groups": [ { "members": [{ "outbound": "vpn1" }, { "outbound": "vpn2" }] }, { "members": [{ "outbound": "wan" }] } ], "retry": { "attempts": 3, "interval_ms": 1000 }, "circuit_breaker": { "failure_threshold": 5, "success_threshold": 2, "timeout_ms": 30000, "half_open_max_requests": 1 } } ] }

data.failover-or-balancing-for-all-internet-t:
Use this to send all internet traffic through an auto-select outbound (urltest or icmptest): if the main link fails, traffic switches to a backup, and with balance it is spread across several links. Add one default_gateway rule for IPv4 and one for IPv6 at the end of the rule list. This works only with nftables; with iptables the configuration fails validation. Addresses on your local network keep normal routing.
By default these rules apply only to traffic from devices on your network. To include the router’s own traffic, turn on General > Settings > General > Process router’s own traffic (iproute.process_router_traffic: true).
config.json{ "iproute": { "process_router_traffic": true }, "route": { "rules": [ { "default_gateway": "ipv4", "outbound": "auto" }, { "default_gateway": "ipv6", "outbound": "auto" } ] } }

data.one-device-or-subnet#One device or subne:
All traffic from the device 192.168.10.20 goes through the VPN. To pick a whole subnet, use it instead: 192.168.10.0/24.
config.json{ "route": { "rules": [ { "src_addr": "192.168.10.20", "outbound": "vpn" } ] } } Conditions can be combined: the rule below sends only addresses from the my_ips list through the VPN, and only from devices in 192.168.10.0/24.
config.json{ "route": { "rules": [ { "list": ["my_ips"], "src_addr": "192.168.10.0/24", "outbound": "vpn" } ] } }
